# Expectation record for 02_constant_expressions/003_static_assert.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_static_assert
area               = 02_constant_expressions
description        = _Static_assert on satisfied conditions across the type system, each corroborated by a printed run-time value
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
THIS PROGRAM CONTAINS NO UNDEFINED AND NO UNSPECIFIED BEHAVIOUR, which is the property both
differential oracles rest on. Undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do anything, so a
divergence between them would prove nothing about either; unspecified behaviour would let two
conforming implementations differ with neither being wrong. Neither is present here.
EVERY ONE OF THE 34 _Static_assert DECLARATIONS IN THIS FILE IS SATISFIED ON ALL FOUR TARGETS, and
that is a soundness requirement rather than a preference. Both mandated oracles compare the
observable behaviour of a binary that was built successfully, so a deliberately failing assertion
would stop translation under both compilers and leave neither oracle anything to compare. Error
detection -- the correct rejection of an invalid program -- is a noted non-goal of this suite for
exactly that reason, so no failing assertion appears anywhere, here or elsewhere in the corpus. Each
assertion is instead paired with a printed line reporting the same property at run time, so a
compiler that accepted the condition while computing the property differently still changes stdout.
EVERY ASSERTION IS TARGET-INVARIANT, which is the one hazard a program of this kind must avoid: an
assertion whose truth depended on a target-varying property would fail to TRANSLATE on some target,
destroying the four-way comparison rather than testing it. Only two kinds of condition therefore
appear. Exact values are asserted solely for quantities identical on all four supported targets --
sizeof of char and of unsigned char (1 apiece), of short, int, long long, float and double (2, 4, 8,
4 and 8), _Alignof of char, short, int and float (1, 2, 4 and 4), sizeof "abc" and sizeof msg (4
apiece, the terminator included), sizeof "" (1), the element count of the macro-bounded table (7)
and its total span as the product TABLE_LEN * sizeof(int), the folded arithmetic 2 + 3 * 4,
(1 << 10) >> 4 and 4294967296LL / 65536, the enumerator values 0, 2, 10 and -1, and 'A' == 65.
Everything else is asserted as a RELATION, which holds whatever the individual values are: the two
size orderings the standard guarantees, sizeof(long) == sizeof(void *), the two header-free width
probes sizeof(sizeof(int)) == sizeof(void *) and sizeof(&table[1] - &table[0]) == sizeof(void *),
the two alignment bounds, that a struct aligns as its strictest member does, that a struct is at
least the sum of its members, and that a union is at least its largest member.
NO ASSERTION DEPENDS ON A TARGET-VARYING PROPERTY. None requires sizeof(long) or sizeof(void *) to
be 8, none names sizeof(long double) at all, none pins _Alignof(double), _Alignof(long long) or
_Alignof(void *), and none observes plain char's signedness -- plain char appears only as a one-byte
size probe, and no signedness can change a size. The measured values behind every one of those
exclusions are recorded in impl_defined_notes below.
NO ARITHMETIC CAN OVERFLOW. The folded expressions are the small constants 2 + 3 * 4, (1 << 10) >> 4
and 4294967296LL / 65536, and each runtime twin recomputes precisely that value from volatile
operands of the same types, so no intermediate leaves the range of int or of long long. Both shift
counts, 10 and 4, are well inside the width of the 32-bit promoted left operand. Both divisions have
a non-zero divisor: 65536 is a literal, and the one division by a MEASURED value -- the table's byte
span by its element stride -- is guarded by a positivity test on both operands, so a zero divisor
cannot reach it even if a measurement were somehow to fail; the guard then prints -1, which keeps
the divergence observable as output instead of becoming a trap.
NO STORAGE IS READ UNINITIALIZED. The ten probe arrays have static storage duration and are
therefore zero-initialized, and their contents are never read at all -- only the byte distance
between two of their element addresses is taken. Every automatic object is initialized in its own
declaration, including the volatile pair array in the local-type probe, whose members are read back
afterwards, and the report struct, which is initialized before its address is passed on.
POINTER ARITHMETIC STAYS IN RANGE, AND THE TWO WIDTH PROBES EVALUATE NOTHING. Both operands of every
subtraction address bytes of one and the same object, so each difference is defined. The two
header-free width probes are operands of sizeof and are therefore UNEVALUATED: sizeof(sizeof(int))
and sizeof(&table[1] - &table[0]) yield the widths of size_t and of ptrdiff_t without the inner
expressions being computed at all, so no subtraction is performed there -- and both element
addresses are in range regardless, the table having seven elements. The single one-past-end pointer
that is genuinely evaluated, table + TABLE_LEN, is formed and subtracted but never dereferenced. The
bounded string scan stops at the array's own size, so it cannot read past the terminator whatever
the array holds. Every reinterpretation of an object's bytes is through a character type, so no
aliasing rule is broken, and nothing depends on padding bytes or on the relative addresses of
unrelated objects.
NO OBJECT IS MODIFIED TWICE BETWEEN SEQUENCE POINTS, no call receives more than one argument capable
of a side effect, and no printed value can depend on the unspecified order in which a call's
arguments are evaluated: every argument of every printf call is a plain read of an object or of a
member, or a pure computation over such reads.
OUTPUT IS FULLY DETERMINISTIC. No address and no pointer value is printed, no timestamp, no
randomness, no locale-dependent formatting; every loop bound is a literal or a measured positive
size; and main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is
truncated by the operating system. The program reads no clock, no environment variable, no file and
no socket -- every input is a literal in this source -- so every cell is reproducible from this
program and this record alone.
NO HEADER IS NAMED ANYWHERE and the single libc prototype the program needs is declared by hand as
`int printf(const char *, ...);`. bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus
stdatomic.h and ships no standard I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so a directive naming
that header would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a) while succeeding on the reference side -- a
spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler under test. _Static_assert and
_Alignof need no header either: both are C11 KEYWORDS, listed among the 44 the lexer recognises in
docs/technical-specifications.md line 497, with line 502 recording the declaration parser that
handles _Static_assert. stdalign.h is therefore deliberately not included; it would only add the
lowercase alignas and alignof spellings, which this program does not use. Every assertion uses the
two-argument form WITH A MESSAGE, because the message-less one-argument form is C23 and the default
gate's -pedantic -Werror rejects it.
THIS RECORD DECLARES NO WARNING-GATE DEVIATION: no ub_audit_flags key appears above, so the program
is audited under the suite's full default gate, which is what area 02 requires of every one of its
eight programs. It was MEASURED clean under -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion
-Wshadow -Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers, and it builds and runs clean
under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all, whose run exits 0
with an empty standard error and with stdout byte-identical to the golden record below. Both gates
are reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline
is untouched. Two details of the local-type probe are what keep the program gate-clean rather than
incidental, and the source file's own header names them for anyone editing it.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
THIS RECORD NARROWS NOTHING. All four targets, all three optimization levels and all three oracles
are enabled, no expected-divergence marker is attached, and the full four-way cross-backend
comparison therefore applies to every line this program prints. These notes exist to record how that
was achieved, because a program of this kind could easily have had to narrow: _Static_assert reaches
straight into the type system, which is precisely where the four supported targets differ.
THE NORMALIZATION IN ONE SENTENCE: a raw value is printed only where it was MEASURED identical on
all four targets, and every property that varies by target is printed as a 0/1 RELATION instead.
NO ADDRESS, NO POINTER VALUE AND NO PLAIN long IS PRINTED ANYWHERE, no sizeof result whose width
differs per target reaches a format string, and the entire format vocabulary of the program is %d
and %lld -- the latter exactly 64 bits wide on every enabled target. Every quantity printed with %d
is an int by its own type or by an explicit cast, so no conversion in a format argument can differ
between the ILP32 backend and the three LP64 backends.
WHY THE ASSERTIONS ARE THE HARDER HALF OF THAT NORMALIZATION. A printed value that varied by target
would merely produce a divergence; a _Static_assert whose condition varied by target would break the
BUILD there, which is worse, because a cell that never runs cannot be compared at all. The asserted
set is therefore restricted to exact quantities measured identical on all four targets and to
relations that hold whatever the individual values are -- ub_notes above enumerates both sets in
full and names every target-varying property the program deliberately does not assert. The
paragraphs below record what each of those properties was measured to be, and why declining to pin
it costs no coverage.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) were MEASURED at 4 bytes with ELF32 on i686 and
at 8 bytes with ELF64 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64, exactly as the per-target table in
docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462 records, so this program asserts and prints only that
long is exactly pointer width, that size_t is exactly pointer width, and that ptrdiff_t is exactly
pointer width -- each true on all four. size_t and ptrdiff_t are probed WITHOUT BEING NAMED, as
sizeof(sizeof(int)) and as sizeof of a pointer difference, because naming either would require
stddef.h and this program includes no header. The runtime twin of that pair compares those
compile-time widths against the pointer size MEASURED as a byte stride, so a backend whose pointer
size disagreed with its own analyser would print 0 rather than agreeing with itself.
sizeof(long double) WAS MEASURED AT 16, 12, 16 AND 16 BYTES across x86-64, i686, AArch64 and
RISC-V 64 -- the x87 80-bit format padded differently against IEEE binary128 -- so it appears only
inside the ordering relation sizeof(double) <= sizeof(long double), and its size is never printed.
The alignments of double and of long long were MEASURED at 8, 4, 8 and 8 across the same four
targets, so they too appear only in relations: that alignment does not decrease with rank, and that
no type aligns more strictly than its own size. Pinning any of these per target is deliberately
another program's job; see the division of labour at the end of these notes.
PLAIN-char SIGNEDNESS was MEASURED as signed on x86-64 and i686 and unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64, so it is never asserted and never observed. Plain char appears only as a one-byte size
probe, whose signedness cannot affect a size, and the one character value that is printed is read
back from an explicitly signed char holding 65 -- a value every char signedness represents exactly.
THE EXECUTION CHARACTER SET IS PINNED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. It is an implementation-defined property
separate from char signedness, and the two must not be confused: routing a character through an
explicitly signed char settles the signedness question and settles nothing about which code the
letter carries. C11 5.2.1 leaves the members of the execution character set and their codes
implementation-defined, so an implementation is free to place A somewhere other than 65. Exactly two
printed lines observe a character's value here -- fold_char_a, which prints (int)'A' from a constant
expression, and run_char_a, which prints the same value back out of the signed char -- and nothing
else in the program observes one: msg is measured only by its size and by the offset of its
terminator, probe_char only by its element size, and no line renders a literal. A single assertion
therefore closes the whole accounting, and the program carries it: _Static_assert('A' == 65, "the
execution character set places A at 65 (ASCII) on this target"). All four enabled targets were
MEASURED to use the same ASCII-family basic execution character set, so the condition holds on every
cell of this record's matrix. The assertion is the mechanism that keeps the two oracles honest: on
an implementation whose character set differed, the cell would FAIL TO TRANSLATE naming the reason
-- accusing the environment, where the difference actually lives -- instead of printing a different
number that oracle (a) or oracle (b) would charge to the compiler under test. A future target that
disagreed would announce itself at the build, and the correct response would be to narrow this
record's target list with the reason recorded here, never to delete the assertion or the two lines
it protects.
TWO IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED LAYOUT FACTS ARE RELIED ON AND WERE VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. struct
triple is int, short, unsigned char and union scalar_bytes has an 8-byte widest member; the standard
leaves their exact sizes to the implementation, and both were MEASURED at 8 bytes on all four
targets, with _Alignof(struct triple) measured at 4 everywhere, which is what licenses printing
those sizes raw. The shapes were chosen for that reason: an aggregate whose padding depended on the
alignment of double or of long long would have sized differently on i686, where those alignments are
4 rather than 8. If a target ever laid these out differently while remaining conforming, the
resulting difference would be an implementation-defined divergence rather than a compiler defect,
and it would have to be recorded as an expected divergence with this record's target list narrowed
and the reason stated here -- never left to surface as a finding against a backend that did nothing
wrong, and never resolved by deleting the aggregate assertions, which would remove coverage this
program exists to provide. The size of the enumerated type is implementation-defined too, and it is
deliberately neither asserted nor printed; only the enumerator values, which their constant
expressions fix, are.
THE DIVISION OF LABOUR, so that nothing this program declines to pin is left untested. Every
width-varying and alignment-varying quantity above is pinned EXACTLY, per target, by
10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c: it selects one expected number from the
architecture predefined macro -- 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64, and 4 on i686 -- and then
asserts _Alignof(double), _Alignof(long long), _Alignof(long), _Alignof(void *), sizeof(void *) and
sizeof(long) against it, so a compiler that chose 5, 6, 7, or the wrong one of 4 and 8, changes that
program's stdout. long double is the one type for which no exact answer exists to assert, its
representation being implementation-defined, so cross-backend VALUE equality for it is a recorded
exclusion in 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.c, while the sibling
004_sizeof_alignof.c in this area bounds it without ever printing its size. Splitting the work this
way is deliberate: it is what lets all three programs keep the full four-target list instead of any
one of them narrowing.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
static_assert_count=34
static_assert_contexts=5
fold_sizes=1 2 4 8 4 8
run_strides=1 2 4 8 4 8
fold_size_order=1 1
run_size_order=1 1
fold_long_is_ptr_width=1
run_long_is_ptr_width=1
fold_width_probes=1 1
run_width_probes=1 1
fold_alignments=1 2 4 4
run_alignments=1 2 4 4
fold_align_bounds=1 1
run_align_bounds=1 1
fold_arith=14 64
run_arith=14 64
fold_wide_arith=65536
run_wide_arith=65536
fold_char_a=65
run_char_a=65
fold_enumerators=0 2 10 -1
run_enumerators=0 2 10 -1
fold_table_len=7
run_table_len=7
fold_string_sizes=4 4 1
run_string_size=4
fold_aggregate_sizes=8 8
run_aggregate_strides=8 8
fold_aggregate_bounds=1 1 1
run_aggregate_bounds=1 1 1
fold_local_type=1 1 110
run_local_type=1 1 110
END
